            <title xml:id="title_id014d502f" xml:lang="da">Som Markens Blomst henvisner</title>
            <title xml:id="title_a9619beb" xml:lang="en">As the Flower in the Field quickly Withers away</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_b515d43c">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_id4cadc311">Niels W. Gade</persName>
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_421142f5">N.F.S. Grundtvig</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_idb8910f76">
               <date isodate="1858" xml:id="date_849b0263">After 1858</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_idf3bbbebd"/>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_id26f5f21c">
               <p xml:id="idm160">Gade uses two different tunes for his setting of N.F.S. Grundtvig's funeral hymn "Som Markens Blomst henvisner": for the first three verses Georg Neumark's "Hvo ikkun lader Herren raade" (He who allows only God to advise him) and for the last two verses he composed a tune of his own. The work was probably written for a funeral in Holmen's Church where Gade was organist from 1858.</p>
            </history>
